var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"usage/#Usage","page":"Usage","text":"The loop process creates the segment and the receiver attaches to it. Both sides then use the same handles: the command ring, one event ring per hardware channel, one snapshot slot per channel, and the loop-wide nav ring with its snapshot.\n\nThe examples on this page use a heap-backed segment (nothing for the path), so they run in one process on any platform. Across two processes, the loop process calls create_segment(path, config) with a file path, conventionally under /dev/shm on Linux, and the receiver calls attach_segment with the same path.\n\nThe constants for event, command and status kinds (EVENT_RECORD, COMMAND_ARM, STATUS_ARMED, ...) are not exported. Qualify them with the module name.\n\nEach band's entry also carries its intermediate frequency: where a signal at zero Doppler sits in the band's samples, including any fixed offset the front end's tuning leaves. Every carrier Doppler in the protocol is relative to it. The loop process writes the band table; the receiver reads it with band_table, for example to acquire around the right frequency.\n\nusing HardwareLoopProtocol\nconst HLP = HardwareLoopProtocol\n\nconfig = SegmentConfig(; channel_count = 2, bands = [BandEntry(:L1, 4e6; intermediate_frequency_hz = -83.0)])\nseg = create_segment(nothing, config)\nband_table(seg)\n1-element Vector{BandEntry}:\n BandEntry(FixedName((0x4c, 0x31, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00)), 4.0e6, -83.0, 1, 1, (0x0000000000000000, 0x0000000000000000))","title":"Usage"},{"category":"section","location":"usage/#Commands:-receiver-loop","page":"Usage","text":"The receiver sends commands with try_publish!. It returns false if the command ring is full, so a command is never lost silently. Each command carries a sequence number, which the loop echoes in its acknowledgement.\n\ncommands = command_ring(seg)\narm = ArmCommand(;\n    signal = :GPSL1CA,\n    prn = 7,\n    carrier_doppler_hz = 1500.0,\n    code_doppler_hz = 1.5,\n    code_phase_chips = 100.5,\n    valid_at_sample = 40_000,\n    tap_sample_shifts = (-2, 0, 2),\n    num_taps = 3,\n    sampling_freq_hz = 4e6,\n)\ntry_publish!(commands, CommandTag(HLP.COMMAND_ARM, 1, 1), arm)\ntrue\n\nThe loop process reads the command with peek! and payload, then consumes it with commit!:\n\nstatus, view, lost = peek!(commands, CommandTag)\nif status !== :empty && view.tag.kind == HLP.COMMAND_ARM\n    received = payload(ArmCommand, commands, view)\n    commit!(commands, view)\n    (Symbol(received.signal), received.prn)\nend\n(:GPSL1CA, 7)","title":"Commands: receiver → loop"},{"category":"section","location":"usage/#Events:-loop-receiver","page":"Usage","text":"The loop process writes events to a channel's event ring with publish!. It never blocks: if the receiver falls behind, the oldest events are overwritten.\n\nevents = event_ring(seg, 1)\npublish!(events, EventTag(HLP.EVENT_STATUS, 1, 40_000; prn = 7),\n         StatusEvent(HLP.STATUS_ARMED, HLP.REJECT_NONE, 40_000, 1))\nfor k = 1:3\n    publish!(events, EventTag(HLP.EVENT_RECORD, 1, 40_000 + 4000k; prn = 7),\n             RecordEvent(1.0 + 0im, 4000, 1, 0, 0.0, 1500.0, 1.5))\nend\nring_available(events)\n4\n\nThe receiver drains the ring. status is :lost when events were overwritten before it read them; lost then says how many.\n\nwhile true\n    status, view, lost = peek!(events, EventTag)\n    status === :empty && break\n    status === :lost && println(\"lost $lost events\")\n    if view.tag.kind == HLP.EVENT_STATUS\n        ack = payload(StatusEvent, events, view)\n        println(\"status $(ack.code) for command $(ack.sequence)\")\n    elseif view.tag.kind == HLP.EVENT_RECORD\n        record = payload(RecordEvent, events, view)\n        println(\"record ending at sample $(view.tag.device_sample): $(record.prompt)\")\n    end\n    commit!(events, view)\nend\n┌ Warning: Assignment to `status` in soft scope is ambiguous because a global variable by the same name exists: `status` will be treated as a new local. Disambiguate by using `local status` to suppress this warning or `global status` to assign to the existing global variable.\n└ @ usage.md:89\n┌ Warning: Assignment to `view` in soft scope is ambiguous because a global variable by the same name exists: `view` will be treated as a new local. Disambiguate by using `local view` to suppress this warning or `global view` to assign to the existing global variable.\n└ @ usage.md:89\n┌ Warning: Assignment to `lost` in soft scope is ambiguous because a global variable by the same name exists: `lost` will be treated as a new local. Disambiguate by using `local lost` to suppress this warning or `global lost` to assign to the existing global variable.\n└ @ usage.md:89\nstatus 1 for command 1\nrecord ending at sample 44000: 1.0 + 0.0im\nrecord ending at sample 48000: 1.0 + 0.0im\nrecord ending at sample 52000: 1.0 + 0.0im","title":"Events: loop → receiver"},{"category":"section","location":"usage/#Snapshots","page":"Usage","text":"A reader that only needs the newest epoch state of a channel can read its snapshot slot instead of draining the event ring.\n\nslot = snapshot_slot(seg, 1)\nstate = EpochStateEvent(1500.0, 1.5, 123.5, 0.25, 2e4, 1499.0, 1.49, 48_000, 13, 0, 1, 0)\nwrite_snapshot!(slot, EventTag(HLP.EVENT_EPOCH_STATE, 1, 44_000; prn = 7), state)\ntag, newest = read_snapshot(slot)\nnewest.carrier_doppler_hz\n1500.0","title":"Snapshots"},{"category":"section","location":"usage/#Navigation","page":"Usage","text":"A loop process that runs vector tracking writes NAV_VECTOR into the header with set_navigation_mode! before it marks itself running, and publishes every navigation cycle on the loop-wide nav_ring: one NavSatelliteEvent per satellite the cycle knows, then the NavSolutionEvent, which commits the cycle. The tags carry channel 0 and the cycle's epoch. publish_nav_solution! also writes the solution into the nav snapshot, which read_nav_snapshot reads. A loop in NAV_NONE (the default) publishes nothing there; the receiver decodes the bits and solves the position itself.\n\nset_navigation_mode!(seg, HLP.NAV_VECTOR)\nepoch = 400_000\npublish!(nav_ring(seg), EventTag(HLP.EVENT_NAV_SATELLITE, 0, epoch; prn = 7),\n         NavSatelliteEvent(1, :GPSL1CA, (2.0e7, 1.0e7, 1.2e7), 0.072, 1.5, -0.1, 45.0, 1,\n                           HLP.NAV_SAT_TRACKED | HLP.NAV_SAT_IN_SOLUTION, HLP.NAV_NOT_RELEASED))\nsolution = NavSolutionEvent(1, (3.9e6, 3.0e5, 5.0e6), (0.0, 0.0, 0.0), 12.5, 1e-7,\n                            7_000_000, 0.25, 4.5, 1.5, 0.0, (2.0f0, 1.8f0, 1.2f0, 1.0f0, 0.7f0),\n                            Int32(5), Int32(6), HLP.NAV_RUNNING | HLP.NAV_SEEDED | HLP.NAV_VALID)\npublish_nav_solution!(seg, EventTag(HLP.EVENT_NAV_SOLUTION, 0, epoch), solution)\nnavigation_mode(seg), read_nav_snapshot(seg)[2].position_ecef_m\n(0x01, (3.9e6, 300000.0, 5.0e6))","title":"Navigation"},{"category":"section","location":"usage/#Liveness","page":"Usage","text":"Each side stamps its heartbeat regularly. The other side checks it with heartbeat_alive.\n\nloop_heartbeat!(seg)\nheartbeat_alive(seg, :loop), heartbeat_alive(seg, :receiver)\n(true, false)\n\nclose(seg)","title":"Liveness"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#API-Reference","page":"API Reference","text":"","title":"API Reference"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Segment","page":"API Reference","text":"","title":"Segment"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Rings","page":"API Reference","text":"","title":"Rings"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Snapshots","page":"API Reference","text":"","title":"Snapshots"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Navigation","page":"API Reference","text":"","title":"Navigation"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Heartbeats,-state-and-process-ids","page":"API Reference","text":"","title":"Heartbeats, state and process ids"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Events","page":"API Reference","text":"","title":"Events"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Commands","page":"API Reference","text":"","title":"Commands"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Names","page":"API Reference","text":"","title":"Names"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#HardwareLoopProtocol.SegmentConfig","page":"API Reference","text":"SegmentConfig(; channel_count, bands, event_capacity, command_capacity, device_index,\n              nav_capacity)\n\nWhat a loop process creates a segment for: how many hardware channels, which bands (a vector of BandEntrys, the first one the reference band), and how deep the rings are. Capacities are rounded up to powers of two; the event capacity defaults to 8192 per channel, eight seconds of records at a 1 kHz fold rate, and the loop-wide nav ring's to 1024 slots, a few seconds of navigation cycles with their satellite events at 10 Hz.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HardwareLoopProtocol.SegmentConfig"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#HardwareLoopProtocol.BandEntry","page":"API Reference","text":"BandEntry(band_id, sampling_freq_hz; intermediate_frequency_hz = 0.0, rf_input = 1, device_index = 1)\n\nOne RF band the loop process serves: its id, the rate its channels' sample counters run at, its intermediate frequency, and the RF input and device it arrives on. Written by the loop process, checked by the receiver against its own band plan.\n\nintermediate_frequency_hz is where a signal at zero Doppler sits in the band's samples: the front end's IF, including any fixed offset its tuning leaves (an RTL-SDR's LO synthesizer lands a fixed number of Hz off the requested frequency). Every carrier Doppler in this protocol is relative to it — a channel's carrier NCO runs at the IF plus the Doppler — so a receiver that acquires from the band's samples searches around it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HardwareLoopProtocol.BandEntry"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#HardwareLoopProtocol.Segment","page":"API Reference","text":"Segment\n\nA mapped protocol segment. Build one with create_segment (the loop process, which owns the layout) or attach_segment (the receiver); close unmaps it. The creator of a file-backed segment may unlink_segment it when the session is over.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HardwareLoopProtocol.Segment"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#HardwareLoopProtocol.create_segment","page":"API Reference","text":"create_segment(path, config::SegmentConfig) -> Segment\n\nCreate (or re-create) the segment at path — a file, conventionally /dev/shm/gnss-loop-<device> — sized and laid out for config, with every ring empty and the header written last. This is the loop process's side. Pass nothing for path to back the segment with a heap buffer instead, for the in-process transport and the tests.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HardwareLoopProtocol.create_segment"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#HardwareLoopProtocol.attach_segment","page":"API Reference","text":"attach_segment(path) -> Segment\n\nMap the segment at path and verify its header: magic, protocol version and layout hash must match this package's, or an ArgumentError names what did not. This is the receiver's side.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HardwareLoopProtocol.attach_segment"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#HardwareLoopProtocol.open_or_attach_segment","page":"API Reference","text":"open_or_attach_segment(path, config; stale_after_ns) -> (segment, attached::Bool)\n\nAttach to the segment at path if one exists whose loop heartbeat is younger than stale_after_ns, otherwise create a fresh one for config. Returns the segment and whether it was attached (true) or created (false).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HardwareLoopProtocol.open_or_attach_segment"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#HardwareLoopProtocol.segment_exists","page":"API Reference","text":"Whether a segment file exists at path.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HardwareLoopProtocol.segment_exists"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#HardwareLoopProtocol.unlink_segment","page":"API Reference","text":"unlink_segment(path)\n\nRemove the segment file. The loop process does this when it shuts down cleanly; a receiver never does.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HardwareLoopProtocol.unlink_segment"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#HardwareLoopProtocol.segment_config","page":"API Reference","text":"segment_config(seg) -> SegmentConfig\n\nThe configuration the segment was created with, read back from its header.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HardwareLoopProtocol.segment_config"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#HardwareLoopProtocol.band_table","page":"API Reference","text":"band_table(seg) -> Vector{BandEntry}\n\nThe bands the loop process serves, the reference band first.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HardwareLoopProtocol.band_table"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/#HardwareLoopProtocol.PROTOCOL_VERSION","page":"API Reference","text":"The protocol revision. A segment whose header carries another is refused.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HardwareLoopProtocol.PROTOCOL_VERSION"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#HardwareLoopProtocol.Ring","page":"API Reference","text":"Ring\n\nA handle on one ring inside a segment: a base pointer plus the geometry, all isbits, so building one costs nothing and it can live in a hot loop's state. Obtain one with command_ring or event_ring.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HardwareLoopProtocol.Ring"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#HardwareLoopProtocol.command_ring","page":"API Reference","text":"The command ring (receiver → loop).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HardwareLoopProtocol.command_ring"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#HardwareLoopProtocol.event_ring","page":"API Reference","text":"Hardware channel channel's event ring (loop → receiver).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HardwareLoopProtocol.event_ring"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#HardwareLoopProtocol.publish!","page":"API Reference","text":"publish!(ring, tag, body) -> UInt64\n\nAppend one slot and return its sequence index. Never blocks: if the consumer is more than a capacity behind, the oldest unconsumed slot is overwritten and the ring's overruns counter is incremented — the consumer will see the gap as lost history. For event rings.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HardwareLoopProtocol.publish!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#HardwareLoopProtocol.try_publish!","page":"API Reference","text":"try_publish!(ring, tag, body) -> Bool\n\nAppend one slot unless that would overwrite an unconsumed one, in which case nothing is written and false is returned. For command rings, where losing an entry silently is the one unacceptable outcome.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HardwareLoopProtocol.try_publish!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#HardwareLoopProtocol.peek!","page":"API Reference","text":"peek!(ring, ::Type{Tag}) -> (status, view::SlotView{Tag}, lost::UInt64)\n\nLook at the consumer's next slot without consuming it. status is :empty (nothing published), :ok (the view is valid) or :lost (the consumer had fallen a whole capacity behind — lost slots were skipped, the ring's consumer_lost counter was advanced and the view is the oldest slot still intact). A slot the producer overwrote while it was being read is skipped and counted the same way, so a caller only ever sees a consistent tag.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HardwareLoopProtocol.peek!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#HardwareLoopProtocol.payload","page":"API Reference","text":"payload(::Type{P}, ring, view) -> Union{Nothing,P}\n\nRead the payload of a peeked slot as a P, or nothing if the producer overwrote the slot in the meantime (the caller then treats the slot as lost and calls commit! to move past it).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HardwareLoopProtocol.payload"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#HardwareLoopProtocol.commit!","page":"API Reference","text":"commit!(ring, view)\n\nConsume the peeked slot: advance the consumer's tail past it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HardwareLoopProtocol.commit!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#HardwareLoopProtocol.ring_head","page":"API Reference","text":"Slots the producer has published so far (its next index).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HardwareLoopProtocol.ring_head"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#HardwareLoopProtocol.ring_tail","page":"API Reference","text":"The consumer's next index.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HardwareLoopProtocol.ring_tail"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#HardwareLoopProtocol.ring_available","page":"API Reference","text":"Slots published and not yet consumed (may exceed the capacity after a lap).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HardwareLoopProtocol.ring_available"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#HardwareLoopProtocol.ring_space","page":"API Reference","text":"Free slots before the producer would overwrite unconsumed ones.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HardwareLoopProtocol.ring_space"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#HardwareLoopProtocol.ring_capacity","page":"API Reference","text":"The number of slots in the ring (a power of two).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HardwareLoopProtocol.ring_capacity"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#HardwareLoopProtocol.consumer_lost","page":"API Reference","text":"Slots the consumer had to skip because they had been overwritten.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HardwareLoopProtocol.consumer_lost"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#HardwareLoopProtocol.producer_overruns","page":"API Reference","text":"Times the producer found the consumer more than a capacity behind.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HardwareLoopProtocol.producer_overruns"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#HardwareLoopProtocol.SnapshotSlot","page":"API Reference","text":"SnapshotSlot\n\nA handle on one channel's seqlock-protected epoch-state snapshot. Obtain it with snapshot_slot.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HardwareLoopProtocol.SnapshotSlot"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#HardwareLoopProtocol.snapshot_slot","page":"API Reference","text":"Hardware channel channel's epoch-state snapshot.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HardwareLoopProtocol.snapshot_slot"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#HardwareLoopProtocol.write_snapshot!","page":"API Reference","text":"write_snapshot!(slot, tag, state)\n\nPublish a new epoch state. The sequence word is odd while the write is in progress and even once it is complete; readers retry on an odd or changed word.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HardwareLoopProtocol.write_snapshot!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#HardwareLoopProtocol.read_snapshot","page":"API Reference","text":"read_snapshot(slot) -> Union{Nothing,Tuple{EventTag,EpochStateEvent}}\n\nThe newest epoch state, or nothing while none has been written. Retries a bounded number of times if the writer is mid-update.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HardwareLoopProtocol.read_snapshot"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#HardwareLoopProtocol.navigation_mode","page":"API Reference","text":"navigation_mode(seg) -> UInt8\n\nWhat the loop process does about the navigation solution: NAV_NONE (it publishes none; the receiver decodes the bits and solves it) or NAV_VECTOR (it runs vector tracking and publishes the solution on the nav_ring). Written by the loop process before it marks itself running.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HardwareLoopProtocol.navigation_mode"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#HardwareLoopProtocol.set_navigation_mode!","page":"API Reference","text":"set_navigation_mode!(seg, mode)\n\nRecord the loop process's navigation mode (see navigation_mode). The loop process sets it once, before set_loop_state! marks it running, whose release store publishes it to a receiver that waits for that state.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HardwareLoopProtocol.set_navigation_mode!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#HardwareLoopProtocol.nav_ring","page":"API Reference","text":"nav_ring(seg) -> Ring\n\nThe loop-wide navigation event ring (loop → receiver): per navigation cycle one NavSatelliteEvent for every satellite the cycle knows, then its NavSolutionEvent, which commits the cycle. Empty unless the loop runs in navigation_mode NAV_VECTOR.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HardwareLoopProtocol.nav_ring"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#HardwareLoopProtocol.publish_nav_solution!","page":"API Reference","text":"publish_nav_solution!(seg, tag::EventTag, solution::NavSolutionEvent)\n\nPublish a navigation cycle's solution: onto the nav ring, after the cycle's satellite events, and into the nav snapshot.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HardwareLoopProtocol.publish_nav_solution!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#HardwareLoopProtocol.read_nav_snapshot","page":"API Reference","text":"read_nav_snapshot(seg) -> Union{Nothing,Tuple{EventTag,NavSolutionEvent}}\n\nThe newest navigation solution, or nothing while none has been published (always, for a loop in NAV_NONE). For a reader that lost the nav ring's history, or only wants \"now\".\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HardwareLoopProtocol.read_nav_snapshot"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#HardwareLoopProtocol.NavSolutionEvent","page":"API Reference","text":"NavSolutionEvent\n\nOne navigation cycle's solution, the last event of the cycle on the nav ring and the value in the nav snapshot. The tag's channel is 0 (loop-wide) and its device_sample the cycle's epoch on the reference band's counter.\n\ncycle — the estimator's navigation-cycle count;\nposition_ecef_m, velocity_ecef_mps — ECEF, metres and metres per second;\nclock_bias_m — the receiver clock bias of the solution's reference system, in metres; clock_drift — the relative clock drift (s/s). Inter-system and inter-frequency biases are not published;\ntime_tai_s, time_tai_frac — the solution's time as whole TAI seconds since J2000 and the fraction of the second, meaningful with NAV_TIME_VALID;\nposition_std_m, clock_std_m — the filter's 1σ uncertainties (NaN while it is not running), time_with_insufficient_meas_s its coasting time;\ndop — GDOP, PDOP, VDOP, HDOP and TDOP, NaN when absent;\nnum_members — satellites in the vector loop, num_sats — satellites in the solution;\nflags — NAV_RUNNING, NAV_SEEDED, NAV_FELL_BACK, NAV_RELEASED, NAV_VALID, NAV_TIME_VALID.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HardwareLoopProtocol.NavSolutionEvent"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#HardwareLoopProtocol.NavSatelliteEvent","page":"API Reference","text":"NavSatelliteEvent\n\nOne satellite as a navigation cycle saw it. A cycle publishes one per satellite it knows, before its NavSolutionEvent; the tag carries the satellite's prn and band, channel 0 and the cycle's epoch.\n\ncycle — the cycle it belongs to; channel — the loop channel that drives the satellite; signal — the signal's id;\nsat_position_ecef_m, sat_time_s (transmit time), residual_m and rate_residual_mps — from the solution, NaN unless NAV_SAT_IN_SOLUTION;\ncn0_dbhz — at the latest epoch the satellite was snapshotted at;\nflags — the NAV_SAT_* bits; release_reason — one of the NAV_RELEASE_* codes, or NAV_NOT_RELEASED.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HardwareLoopProtocol.NavSatelliteEvent"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#HardwareLoopProtocol.loop_heartbeat!","page":"API Reference","text":"Stamp the loop process's heartbeat with time_ns() (monotonic within the host).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HardwareLoopProtocol.loop_heartbeat!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#HardwareLoopProtocol.receiver_heartbeat!","page":"API Reference","text":"Stamp the receiver's heartbeat.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HardwareLoopProtocol.receiver_heartbeat!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#HardwareLoopProtocol.loop_heartbeat","page":"API Reference","text":"The loop process's last heartbeat in time_ns() nanoseconds, or 0 if never stamped.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HardwareLoopProtocol.loop_heartbeat"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#HardwareLoopProtocol.receiver_heartbeat","page":"API Reference","text":"The receiver's last heartbeat in time_ns() nanoseconds, or 0 if never stamped.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HardwareLoopProtocol.receiver_heartbeat"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#HardwareLoopProtocol.heartbeat_alive","page":"API Reference","text":"heartbeat_alive(seg, side; stale_after_ns = 500_000_000, now_ns = time_ns()) -> Bool\n\nWhether side (:loop or :receiver) has heartbeated within stale_after_ns. A heartbeat of zero (never stamped) is not alive.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HardwareLoopProtocol.heartbeat_alive"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#HardwareLoopProtocol.loop_state","page":"API Reference","text":"loop_state(seg) -> UInt64\n\nThe loop process's lifecycle state, one of HardwareLoopProtocol.LOOP_STATE_STARTING (set by create_segment), LOOP_STATE_RUNNING, LOOP_STATE_STOPPING, LOOP_STATE_STOPPED or LOOP_STATE_FAULT.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HardwareLoopProtocol.loop_state"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#HardwareLoopProtocol.set_loop_state!","page":"API Reference","text":"Publish the loop process's lifecycle state; see loop_state.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HardwareLoopProtocol.set_loop_state!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#HardwareLoopProtocol.loop_pid","page":"API Reference","text":"The process id the loop process recorded with set_loop_pid!.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HardwareLoopProtocol.loop_pid"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#HardwareLoopProtocol.receiver_pid","page":"API Reference","text":"The process id the receiver recorded with set_receiver_pid!.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HardwareLoopProtocol.receiver_pid"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#HardwareLoopProtocol.set_loop_pid!","page":"API Reference","text":"Record the loop process's process id in the header.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HardwareLoopProtocol.set_loop_pid!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#HardwareLoopProtocol.set_receiver_pid!","page":"API Reference","text":"Record the receiver's process id in the header.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HardwareLoopProtocol.set_receiver_pid!"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#HardwareLoopProtocol.EventTag","page":"API Reference","text":"EventTag\n\nThe 16-byte tag every event carries after its sequence word: what kind of event it is, which hardware channel, band and satellite it belongs to, and the device sample it refers to (a record's end, an epoch boundary, the sample a status change took effect at).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HardwareLoopProtocol.EventTag"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#HardwareLoopProtocol.RecordEvent","page":"API Reference","text":"RecordEvent\n\nWhat Tracking._apply_correlator_output needs of a record and nothing more — the complete history the receiver's lock detectors and decoder rest on: the filtered prompt, the record's length, the blocks it credits to the bit clock, the C/N₀ after it, and the NCO words it really ran under.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HardwareLoopProtocol.RecordEvent"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#HardwareLoopProtocol.BitEvent","page":"API Reference","text":"BitEvent\n\nOne navigation soft bit as the bit buffer emitted it: its sign is the hard decision, its magnitude the confidence. bit_index counts bits since the channel's bit clock was last (re)started.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HardwareLoopProtocol.BitEvent"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#HardwareLoopProtocol.EpochStateEvent","page":"API Reference","text":"EpochStateEvent\n\n\"Newest wins\": the channel's loop state at the common fold boundary the tag's device_sample names — the Dopplers, the absolute code phase referenced to that boundary, the carrier phase, the sync state and block count, and the NCO words in effect. PVT and the dashboard read this; the same value is written into the channel's snapshot slot.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HardwareLoopProtocol.EpochStateEvent"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#HardwareLoopProtocol.StatusEvent","page":"API Reference","text":"StatusEvent\n\nA command acknowledgement (\"armed at device sample S\", \"rejected, reason R\") or a channel or loop status change. sequence is the command's sequence number for an acknowledgement, or the event sequence from which history was lost for STATUS_HISTORY_LOST. STATUS_CHANNEL_STATE carries the full seed a receiver needs to adopt the channel's satellite after re-attaching: signal, group, PRN, Dopplers and code phase at sample.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HardwareLoopProtocol.StatusEvent"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#HardwareLoopProtocol.TapsEvent","page":"API Reference","text":"TapsEvent\n\nThe full correlator accumulators of one record, latest tap first, antenna-major (taps[(antenna - 1) * num_taps + tap]), on the host's amplitude scale. Only published for channels configured with want_taps (the dashboard, a replay recording, a future vector-tracking path).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HardwareLoopProtocol.TapsEvent"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#HardwareLoopProtocol.CommandTag","page":"API Reference","text":"CommandTag\n\nThe 16-byte tag every command carries: its kind, the channel it addresses (0 for the whole loop) and the receiver's sequence number, which the acknowledging status event echoes.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HardwareLoopProtocol.CommandTag"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#HardwareLoopProtocol.ArmCommand","page":"API Reference","text":"ArmCommand\n\n\"Arm channel k with this configuration\": GNSSReceiver's HardwareChannelConfig with every Julia object replaced by a plain value — the signal and group ids as fixed names, the tap offsets as a fixed tuple. The loop executes the arm; the allocation policy stays with the receiver.\n\ncarrier_loop_bandwidth_hz / code_loop_bandwidth_hz of 0 mean the signal's defaults. valid_at_sample is on the channel's band counter, like every sample in this protocol.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HardwareLoopProtocol.ArmCommand"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#HardwareLoopProtocol.ConfigureCommand","page":"API Reference","text":"ConfigureCommand\n\nLoop-wide configuration: the fold epoch, the predicted commit lead, the coherent accumulation ceiling, the record integration ceiling and which events are published. Zero leaves a field unchanged.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HardwareLoopProtocol.ConfigureCommand"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#HardwareLoopProtocol.ReleaseCommand","page":"API Reference","text":"Release channel k. Carries nothing beyond its tag.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HardwareLoopProtocol.ReleaseCommand"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#HardwareLoopProtocol.ShutdownCommand","page":"API Reference","text":"Stop the loop process. Carries nothing beyond its tag.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HardwareLoopProtocol.ShutdownCommand"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#HardwareLoopProtocol.QueryStateCommand","page":"API Reference","text":"Ask for a STATUS_CHANNEL_STATE event on every armed channel (adoption).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HardwareLoopProtocol.QueryStateCommand"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#HardwareLoopProtocol.FixedName","page":"API Reference","text":"FixedName(s)\n\nA fixed-length, zero-padded ASCII name — how a Symbol such as :GPSL1CA or a tracking-group key crosses the segment, since neither side can share a symbol table. String(name) / Symbol(name) recover it; a name longer than NAME_BYTES is an ArgumentError when built on the receiver side.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HardwareLoopProtocol.FixedName"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/#HardwareLoopProtocol.NAME_BYTES","page":"API Reference","text":"Bytes reserved for a fixed-length ASCII name (a signal or group id).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HardwareLoopProtocol.NAME_BYTES"},{"category":"section","location":"#HardwareLoopProtocol.jl","page":"Home","text":"","title":"HardwareLoopProtocol.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"#Versioning","page":"Home","text":"The public API is every exported symbol, listed in the API Reference. It follows semantic versioning: breaking changes to these symbols bump the major version.\n\nThe memory layout of the segment is versioned separately. Every segment header carries PROTOCOL_VERSION and a hash of the record layouts, and attach_segment refuses a segment whose version or layout does not match. The receiver and the loop process must therefore be built against compatible versions of this package.","title":"Versioning"},{"category":"module","location":"#HardwareLoopProtocol","page":"Home","text":"HardwareLoopProtocol\n\nThe shared-memory protocol between a GNSS receiver process and the allocation-free loop process that closes the tracking loops of a hardware correlator.\n\nThe two processes share one memory segment — a file, conventionally under /dev/shm on Linux, mapped by both — and nothing else: no serialisation, no syscalls on the hot path, no futex. The segment holds\n\na header (magic, protocol version, layout hash, channel count, the band table and both sides' heartbeats), refused on any mismatch;\none command ring (receiver → loop): arm, release, configure, shutdown, each acknowledged by a status event in the channel's ring;\none loop-wide nav ring (loop → receiver) of navigation-solution and per-satellite events, plus a seqlock snapshot of the newest solution, used when the loop runs vector tracking (navigation_mode);\nper hardware channel one event ring (loop → receiver) of fixed-size tagged events in loop order — records, bits, epoch states, status — plus a seqlock snapshot of the newest epoch state for readers that only want \"now\".\n\nEvery ring is single-producer single-consumer. Producers never block: a full event ring overwrites its oldest entry and the consumer learns how much history it lost from the sequence numbers; a full command ring refuses the command (try_publish! returns false) — a command must never be lost silently. Head and tail are 64-bit counters written with release and read with acquire ordering through Julia's atomic pointer intrinsics; every slot and the snapshot carry a sequence word so a torn read is detected rather than believed.\n\nThe package depends on nothing but Base and is written so that a --trim=safe build of the loop process can carry it: no strings are built on the hot path, every record is isbits, and the mapping is done with plain ccalls.\n\nThe same code serves an in-process transport: create_segment with nothing for the path backs the segment with a heap buffer, so the simulated device tests and the software-only CI path run the very code the binary runs.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HardwareLoopProtocol"}]
}
